{% extends "base.html.j2" %}

{% block head %}
<title>Forex Partial-Correlation Network Report — {{ report_date }}</title>
<style>
  .front-matter {
    background: #f0f4ff;
    border-left: 4px solid #2a78d6;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .front-matter h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; color: #17439c; }
  .params { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem 1.5rem; margin: .5rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .params li { font-size: .9rem; }
  .params .label { color: #6f6e6a; margin-right: .3rem; }
  .plot-pair { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; justify-content: center; }
  .plot-pair figure { flex: 1 1 260px; }
  .summary { background: #f9f9f9; border: 1px solid #e8e8e8; border-radius: 4px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; }
  .about { background: #f9f9f9; border: 1px solid #e8e8e8; border-radius: 4px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; }
  .about h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; color: #111; }
  .about dl { margin: .75rem 0 0; }
  .about dt { font-weight: 600; color: #111; }
  .about dd { margin: 0 0 .6rem; color: #333; }
  .about dd p { margin: 0; }
  .about dd p + p { margin-top: .5rem; }
  .toc { background: #f9f9f9; border: 1px solid #e8e8e8; border-radius: 4px;
         padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem; }
  .toc h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; color: #111; }
  .toc ul { margin: .4rem 0 0; }
</style>
{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<h1>Forex Partial-Correlation Network Report</h1>
<p class="meta">Generated: {{ updated }}</p>

<div class="front-matter">
  <h2>Report date: {{ report_date }}</h2>
  <p class="meta">Data reflects the most recent date present in the source tables.</p>
  <ul class="params">
    <li><span class="label">Window days:</span>{{ params.window_days }}</li>
    <li><span class="label">Step days:</span>{{ params.step_days }}</li>
    <li><span class="label">Min observations:</span>{{ params.min_observations }}</li>
    <li><span class="label">Max lag:</span>{{ params.max_lag }}</li>
    <li><span class="label">FDR alpha:</span>{{ params.fdr_alpha }}</li>
    <li><span class="label">Granularity:</span>{{ params.granularity }}</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<nav class="toc">
  <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#about">About this report</a></li>
    <li><a href="#network-graph">Network graph</a></li>
    <li><a href="#recent-data">Most recent density data</a></li>
    <li><a href="#recent-direction-changes">Most recent direction changes</a></li>
    <li><a href="#distributions">Distributions — full history</a></li>
    <li><a href="#trends">Trends — last year</a></li>
    <li><a href="#summary">Qualitative summary</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>

<div class="about" id="about">
  <h2>About this report</h2>
  <p>This report tracks a statistical network built from the returns of the 7 major FX
    pairs. For each date, every pair of currencies is checked for a direct relationship
    with every other pair — the result is a graph where nodes are currency pairs and
    edges are the relationships that date's data actually supports. The measurements
    below are what the rest of this report is built from:</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>Partial correlation</dt>
    <dd><p>How strongly two pairs move together <em>after</em> netting out the influence of
      the other five — it isolates a direct relationship from one that only shows up
      because both pairs are being pushed around by some third pair they share.</p>
      <p><strong>Reading it:</strong> the sign tells you the direction of co-movement — positive
      means the two pairs tend to move the same way, negative means opposite. The magnitude
      tells you how strong that link is: values near zero are real but weak (the model kept
      the edge, but only just), while values approaching the plots' saturation ceiling
      (|0.6|) are about as strong as this dataset tends to produce. A pair with <em>no</em>
      edge at all isn't "weak" — the model found no direct link once the other five pairs
      are accounted for, often because both are only related through a shared driver
      (commonly a broad dollar move).</p></dd>

    <dt>Granger causality</dt>
    <dd><p>A test for whether one pair's past values help predict another pair's future
      values, beyond what that pair's own history already explains. It's used to give
      each edge a direction — which pair tends to move first.</p>
      <p><strong>Reading it:</strong> a direction means real statistical evidence of a lead-lag
      relationship, not just "these move together." Treat a bidirected result (both
      directions significant) with a little extra skepticism — fast, simultaneous reactions
      to shared news can produce that pattern even without a genuine two-way feedback loop.
      And a single day's direction is a noisy read on its own; it's worth checking whether
      it's held up over the last few days (see "Most recent direction changes" below) rather
      than trusting one date in isolation.</p></dd>

    <dt>Density</dt>
    <dd><p>The fraction of all possible pairings among the 7 majors (21 total) that have an
      active edge on a given date — a rough gauge of how broadly interconnected the
      market is that day.</p>
      <p><strong>Reading it:</strong> rising density usually means the majors are becoming more
      interconnected — often a sign of market stress or a single dominant macro theme (a
      broad risk-on/risk-off move, a shared dollar driver) crowding out pair-specific
      behavior. Falling density usually means pairs are behaving more independently — a
      calmer market, or one where pair-specific news is driving prices instead of a shared
      theme.</p></dd>

    <dt>Mean absolute partial correlation</dt>
    <dd><p>The average <em>strength</em> of that date's active edges, as opposed to how many
      there are. Density and this measure can move in opposite directions — e.g. more
      pairs becoming linked while each individual link gets weaker.</p>
      <p><strong>Reading it:</strong> a day with many <em>weak</em> edges (high density, low
      mean |partial corr|) is a different regime from a day with a few <em>very strong</em>
      ones (low density, high mean |partial corr|) — the first looks like broad, mild
      co-movement across the majors; the second looks like a small cluster of pairs reacting
      intensely to the same thing. Read this measurement alongside density, not by itself.</p></dd>

    <dt>Direction</dt>
    <dd><p>Each edge is labeled <code>i-&gt;j</code> or <code>j-&gt;i</code> (one pair leads
      the other), <code>i&lt;-&gt;j</code> (bidirected — both directions found
      significant), or <code>undirected</code> (the pairs are linked, but no lead-lag
      relationship was significant either way).</p>
      <p><strong>Reading it:</strong> a change from one state to another (see the direction-changes
      table below) is worth asking "what changed?" — a new edge often means a new shared
      driver, a lost edge often means one pair had idiosyncratic news that broke the prior
      co-movement, and swapped leadership (<code>i-&gt;j</code> to <code>j-&gt;i</code>) is
      worth checking against which currency's news calendar was actually active that day. The
      single biggest caveat: a lone, one-day flip is more likely statistical noise than a real
      regime change — Granger tests sitting near the FDR significance boundary can flicker in
      and out from one window to the next just from sampling noise. Trust a flip once it
      persists across a few consecutive dates, not the moment it first appears.</p></dd>
  </dl>
  <p>For more on the project behind this report, read the blog post
    <a href="https://badassdatascience.substack.com/p/our-heroine-wiretaps-seven-currency"
       target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our Heroine Wiretaps Seven Currency Pairs</a>; the
    source code that produced it is on
    <a href="https://github.com/badass-data-science/forex-partial-correlation-network"
       target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.</p>
</div>

<section id="network-graph">
  <h2>Network graph — {{ report_date }}</h2>
  <div id="d3-graph"></div>
  <p class="meta">Blue = positive, red = negative partial correlation; width scales with
    |partial correlation|; arrows mark Granger-significant direction. Drag a node to rearrange it.</p>
  <script>{{ d3_js | safe }}</script>
  <script>
  (function () {
    var graph = {{ d3_graph_data | safe }};
    // width/height are just working room for the simulation to settle in --
    // generous on purpose so forceLink.distance()/forceManyBody.strength()
    // (below) have space to push nodes apart. The *displayed* canvas is fit
    // tightly to wherever the nodes actually end up (see the 'end' handler),
    // so there's no leftover empty margin baked into a fixed box size, and
    // nodes render at whatever size that tight fit implies -- typically
    // larger than they would in an oversized fixed canvas, not smaller.
    var width = 980, height = 820, nodeRadius = 34;
    var posColor = [42, 120, 214], negColor = [227, 73, 72], midColor = [185, 184, 178];
    var saturationCeiling = 0.6;

    function mixColor(pcorr) {
      var t = Math.min(Math.abs(pcorr) / saturationCeiling, 1);
      var pole = pcorr >= 0 ? posColor : negColor;
      var rgb = midColor.map(function (m, i) { return Math.round(m + (pole[i] - m) * t); });
      return 'rgb(' + rgb.join(',') + ')';
    }
    function edgeWidth(pcorr) {
      return 1.4 + Math.min(Math.abs(pcorr) / saturationCeiling, 1) * 4.6;
    }
    function pointNear(from, to, r) {
      var dx = to.x - from.x, dy = to.y - from.y;
      var len = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy) || 1;
      return { x: to.x - (dx / len) * r, y: to.y - (dy / len) * r };
    }

    var svg = d3.select('#d3-graph').append('svg')
        .attr('viewBox', '0 0 ' + width + ' ' + height)
        .attr('width', '100%')
        .attr('height', height)
        .style('background', '#fcfcfb')
        .style('border', '1px solid #e8e8e8')
        .style('border-radius', '4px');

    var defs = svg.append('defs');
    graph.links.forEach(function (d, i) {
      defs.append('marker')
          .attr('id', 'd3-arrow-' + i)
          .attr('viewBox', '0 -5 10 10')
          .attr('refX', 9)
          .attr('markerWidth', 6)
          .attr('markerHeight', 6)
          .attr('orient', 'auto-start-reverse')
        .append('path')
          .attr('d', 'M0,-5L10,0L0,5')
          .attr('fill', mixColor(d.partial_corr));
    });

    var simulation = d3.forceSimulation(graph.nodes)
        .force('link', d3.forceLink(graph.links).id(function (d) { return d.id; })
          .distance(340).strength(0.4))
        .force('charge', d3.forceManyBody().strength(-950))
        .force('center', d3.forceCenter(width / 2, height / 2))
        .force('collide', d3.forceCollide(nodeRadius + 6));

    var link = svg.append('g').selectAll('line')
        .data(graph.links)
        .join('line')
          .attr('stroke', function (d) { return mixColor(d.partial_corr); })
          .attr('stroke-width', function (d) { return edgeWidth(d.partial_corr); })
          .attr('marker-end', function (d, i) {
            return (d.arrow === 'forward' || d.arrow === 'both') ? 'url(#d3-arrow-' + i + ')' : null;
          })
          .attr('marker-start', function (d, i) {
            return d.arrow === 'both' ? 'url(#d3-arrow-' + i + ')' : null;
          });

    var linkLabel = svg.append('g').selectAll('text')
        .data(graph.links.filter(function (d) { return d.label; }))
        .join('text')
          .attr('font-size', 10)
          .attr('font-family', 'Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif')
          .attr('fill', '#0b0b0b')
          .attr('text-anchor', 'middle')
          .text(function (d) { return (d.partial_corr >= 0 ? '+' : '') + d.partial_corr.toFixed(2); });

    var node = svg.append('g').selectAll('g')
        .data(graph.nodes)
        .join('g')
          .call(d3.drag()
            .on('start', function (event, d) {
              if (!event.active) simulation.alphaTarget(0.3).restart();
              d.fx = d.x; d.fy = d.y;
            })
            .on('drag', function (event, d) { d.fx = event.x; d.fy = event.y; })
            .on('end', function (event, d) {
              if (!event.active) simulation.alphaTarget(0);
              d.fx = null; d.fy = null;
            }));

    node.append('circle')
        .attr('r', nodeRadius)
        .attr('fill', '#fcfcfb')
        .attr('stroke', '#0b0b0b')
        .attr('stroke-width', 1.2);

    node.append('text')
        .text(function (d) { return d.id; })
        .attr('text-anchor', 'middle')
        .attr('dy', '0.32em')
        .attr('font-size', 11)
        .attr('font-family', 'Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif')
        .attr('fill', '#0b0b0b');

    simulation.on('tick', function () {
      link.each(function (d) {
        var start = pointNear(d.target, d.source, nodeRadius);
        var end = pointNear(d.source, d.target, nodeRadius);
        d3.select(this).attr('x1', start.x).attr('y1', start.y).attr('x2', end.x).attr('y2', end.y);
      });
      linkLabel
          .attr('x', function (d) { return (d.source.x + d.target.x) / 2; })
          .attr('y', function (d) { return (d.source.y + d.target.y) / 2; });
      node.attr('transform', function (d) { return 'translate(' + d.x + ',' + d.y + ')'; });
    });

    // Once the simulation settles, crop the viewBox tightly to wherever the
    // nodes actually landed (plus a margin for their radius/labels) instead
    // of leaving the full working width x height visible -- removes the dead
    // space around a compact layout without rescaling node/text sizes
    // relative to it, so the graph reads bigger, not smaller.
    simulation.on('end', function () {
      var margin = nodeRadius + 30;
      var xs = graph.nodes.map(function (d) { return d.x; });
      var ys = graph.nodes.map(function (d) { return d.y; });
      var minX = Math.min.apply(null, xs) - margin;
      var maxX = Math.max.apply(null, xs) + margin;
      var minY = Math.min.apply(null, ys) - margin;
      var maxY = Math.max.apply(null, ys) + margin;
      var boxWidth = maxX - minX;
      var boxHeight = maxY - minY;
      var renderedWidth = svg.node().getBoundingClientRect().width || width;

      svg.attr('viewBox', minX + ' ' + minY + ' ' + boxWidth + ' ' + boxHeight)
          .attr('height', Math.round(renderedWidth * boxHeight / boxWidth));
    });
  })();
  </script>

  <h3>All edges — {{ report_date }}</h3>
  {% if latest_edge_rows %}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Pair i</th>
        <th>Pair j</th>
        <th>Partial corr</th>
        <th>Direction</th>
        <th>Granger p (i&rarr;j)</th>
        <th>Granger p (j&rarr;i)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in latest_edge_rows %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.pair_i }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.pair_j }}</td>
        <td>{{ '%+.4f' | format(row.partial_corr) }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.direction | replace('_', ' ') }}</td>
        <td>{{ '%.4f' | format(row.granger_p_i_to_j) }}</td>
        <td>{{ '%.4f' | format(row.granger_p_j_to_i) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% else %}
  <p class="no-data">No edges survived the lasso penalty on {{ report_date }}.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="recent-data">
  <h2>Most recent density data</h2>
  {% if recent_density_rows %}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Date</th>
        <th>Edge count</th>
        <th>Density</th>
        <th>Mean |partial corr|</th>
        <th>Directed</th>
        <th>Bidirected</th>
        <th>Undirected</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in recent_density_rows %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.date }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.edge_count }}</td>
        <td>{{ '%.4f' | format(row.density) }}</td>
        <td>{{ '%.4f' | format(row.mean_abs_partial_corr) }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.directed_edge_count }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.bidirected_edge_count }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.undirected_edge_count }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% else %}
  <p class="no-data">No density data available.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="recent-direction-changes">
  <h2>Most recent direction changes</h2>
  {% if recent_flip_rows %}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Date</th>
        <th>Pair i</th>
        <th>Pair j</th>
        <th>Previous direction</th>
        <th>New direction</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in recent_flip_rows %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.date }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.pair_i }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.pair_j }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.previous_direction | replace('_', ' ') }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.new_direction | replace('_', ' ') }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% else %}
  <p class="no-data">No direction changes recorded.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="distributions">
  <h2>Distributions — full history</h2>
  <figure>
    <img src="{{ boxplot_data_uri }}" alt="Boxplots of density and mean |partial correlation| across the full time series">
    <figcaption>Density and mean |partial correlation|, across every date in the source data.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="trends">
  <h2>Trends — last year</h2>
  <figure>
    <img src="{{ timeseries_data_uri }}" alt="Time series of density and mean |partial correlation| over the last year">
    <figcaption>Density and mean |partial correlation|, trailing 365 days from {{ report_date }}.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="summary">
  <h2>Qualitative summary</h2>
  {% if llm_summary %}
  <p class="warn">The following summary was generated by an LLM using the tables and
    statistics above. Unlike the rest of this report, this section is not a direct
    readout of the data. Please apply judgement before acting on this interpretation.
    <strong>Caveat emptor!</strong></p>
  <div class="summary">
    {% for paragraph in llm_summary.narrative.split('\n\n') %}
    <p>{{ paragraph }}</p>
    {% endfor %}
    {% if llm_summary.bullets %}
    <h3>Strategic takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      {% for bullet in llm_summary.bullets %}
      <li>{{ bullet }}</li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% elif llm_summary_enabled %}
  <p class="no-data">LLM summary unavailable (the call failed or returned no content) — see logs.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p class="no-data">LLM summary skipped (--no-llm-summary).</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>
{% endblock %}
